{"meta": {"course": "cat100-supplement", "chapter": "03", "section": "03", "chapter_title": "Data Storytelling With Excel", "title": "Charts That Answer Questions", "description": "CAT 100 textbook section connecting charts that answer questions to Excel pivot-table finding with unit labels, source note, and claim-first chart title, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.", "learning_objectives": ["Analyze how the Excel pivot-table finding with unit labels, source note, and claim-first chart title shows a defensible decision in CAT 100.", "Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.", "Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.", "Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it."], "concept_ids": ["cat100_supplement_data_storytelling_with_excel", "cat100_supplement_charts_that_answer_questions", "evidence_based_revision", "ai_supported_learning"], "estimated_time_minutes": 14, "order": 3, "top_tier_hardened": false, "prereq_section_ids": ["cat100-supplement/03/02"], "practice_ids": ["cat100_supplement_3_3_charts_that_answer_questions_artifact_signal", "cat100_supplement_3_3_charts_that_answer_questions_annotation_signal", "cat100_supplement_3_3_charts_that_answer_questions_ai_boundary", "cat100_supplement_3_3_charts_that_answer_questions_calibration"]}, "content": "# Charts That Answer Questions\n\n![Charts That Answer Questions textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat100-supplement/03-03-charts-that-answer-questions.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nA chart is not decoration added to finished data; it is an argument. Before you pick a chart type, you have to know the question the chart is supposed to answer, because the question determines the shape. \"Which region sold the most?\" is a comparison and wants bars. \"How did sales change over the year?\" is a trend and wants a line. Choosing the chart before the question is how people end up with a pie chart that hides the very comparison they meant to show.\n\nThis section also teaches the honest-axis rule, the single most abused setting in everyday charts. Where a value axis starts decides how large a difference looks, and starting it above zero can make a small gap look like a landslide. Reading and building charts that answer a question truthfully is the skill here, not making them colorful.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the Excel pivot-table finding with unit labels, source note, and claim-first chart title shows a defensible decision in CAT 100.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nYou have four regions with Q1 revenue: West 48000, East 31000, South 29000, North 22000. You highlight the data, click the chart button, and Excel hands you a default column chart titled \"Q1 Revenue.\" It is technically a chart, but it answers nothing on its own. A viewer still has to do the work of deciding what the bars mean.\n\nNow compare two finishing moves on the same chart. In one, the title stays \"Q1 Revenue\" and the axis quietly starts at 20000, so North's bar nearly disappears and West looks five times larger than it is. In the other, the axis starts at 0 and the title reads \"West Led Q1 Revenue, More Than Double North.\" Same data, two very different impressions. The difference between them is the subject of this section: a chart that answers a question honestly versus one that merely exists.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nThe chart type follows from the kind of question, and there is a short, reliable map between them:\n\n| Question type | Right chart | Why |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Compare separate categories | Bar or column | Length is easy to compare side by side |\n| Show change over time | Line | The connected slope reads as a trend |\n| Show parts of one whole | Pie (sparingly) | Only when slices sum to 100% and there are few of them |\n| Relate two numeric variables | Scatter | Position shows correlation |\n\nMost everyday business questions are category comparisons, so bars are the default and pies are the exception. A pie fails the moment you have more than four or five slices or want a precise comparison, because the eye reads bar length far more accurately than slice angle.\n\nTwo principles keep a chart honest. First, the value axis on a bar or column chart must start at zero, because bar length is the visual encoding and a truncated axis exaggerates differences. Second, the title should state the finding, not just label the data. \"West Led Q1 at 48K\" is a claim-first title; \"Q1 Revenue\" is only a caption. A claim-first title turns the chart into evidence for a specific statement a reader can check against the bars.\n\n## Worked Example\n\nSuppose you have quarterly revenue by region and you want a chart that answers one question.\n\n```\n   A        B\n1  Region   Q1 Revenue\n2  West     48000\n3  East     31000\n4  South    29000\n5  North    22000\n```\n\nStep 1. State the question first: \"Which region led Q1 revenue?\" That is a comparison across separate categories, so the right chart is a bar (or column) chart, not a line or pie chart.\n\nStep 2. Build the chart from A2:B5. Each region gets one bar. Make sure the value axis starts at 0, so West (48000) reads as roughly twice North (22000), which matches the data. If the axis started at 20000, North's bar would nearly vanish and the gap would look far larger than it is.\n\nStep 3. Replace the default title 'Q1 Revenue' with a claim-first title: 'West Led Q1 Revenue at 48K, More Than Double North'. Now the title states the finding and the bars are the evidence.\n\nThe auditable decision chain: question -> chart type (bar for category comparison) -> honest axis (start at 0) -> claim-first title. A reader can check each link against the four numbers above.\n\nMatch each question type to the chart it calls for, then work the finishing-move dilemma below.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"match\" items='[{\"term\":\"Which region sold the most? (compare categories)\",\"definition\":\"Bar or column chart\"},{\"term\":\"How did sales change over the year? (change over time)\",\"definition\":\"Line chart\"},{\"term\":\"What share of total is each of 3 parts? (parts of a whole)\",\"definition\":\"Pie chart (sparingly)\"},{\"term\":\"Do study hours relate to exam scores? (two numeric variables)\",\"definition\":\"Scatter plot\"}]'></sequence-builder>\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"Finishing the Q1 revenue chart honestly\",\"start\":\"n1\",\"nodes\":{\"n1\":{\"prompt\":\"You have West 48K, East 31K, South 29K, North 22K and a default column chart titled Q1 Revenue. First decision: where should the value axis start?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Start the axis at 20000 so the differences look bigger\",\"next\":\"trunc\",\"feedback\":\"A truncated axis is not styling; it visually triples a real gap.\"},{\"label\":\"Start the axis at 0 so bar length is proportional\",\"next\":\"axis0\",\"feedback\":\"Correct: bar length is the encoding, so it must start at zero.\"}]},\"trunc\":{\"prompt\":\"With the axis at 20000, North nearly vanishes and West looks five times East. A reviewer flags it. What now?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Reset the axis to 0\",\"next\":\"axis0\",\"feedback\":\"Good recovery: the bars are now proportional to the numbers.\"},{\"label\":\"Keep it; it makes the story punchier\",\"next\":\"bad\",\"feedback\":\"This misleads anyone who trusts the bars. Not defensible.\"}]},\"axis0\":{\"prompt\":\"Axis is honest. Now the title. Which one turns the chart into checkable evidence?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Keep Q1 Revenue\",\"next\":\"caption\",\"feedback\":\"That is only a caption; it states no finding.\"},{\"label\":\"West Led Q1 Revenue at 48K, More Than Double North\",\"next\":\"win\",\"feedback\":\"A claim-first title the bars can verify.\"}]},\"win\":{\"prompt\":\"Done: bar chart, axis at 0, claim-first title. A skeptic can confirm West is roughly twice North from the bars alone.\",\"feedback\":\"This is the auditable chart: question -> bar -> honest axis -> claim-first title.\",\"choices\":[]},\"caption\":{\"prompt\":\"A caption leaves the viewer to do the analytical work. The chart exists but answers nothing on its own.\",\"feedback\":\"Restate the title as the finding the bars support.\",\"choices\":[]},\"bad\":{\"prompt\":\"The chart now argues a conclusion the data does not support.\",\"feedback\":\"A misleading axis fails the honesty check; reset to zero.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nWrite the question before you build the chart, then let the question pick the chart type and the title. If you cannot state the one question your chart answers, you are decorating data, not analyzing it.\n\nRun a chart through this three-point honesty check before sharing it:\n\n1. **Type check:** Does the chart type match the question (bars for category comparison, line for change over time)?\n2. **Axis check:** Does the value axis start at zero so bar lengths are proportional to the numbers?\n3. **Title check:** Does the title state the finding the bars support, not just name the dataset?\n\nIf a reader could read the opposite conclusion from your chart than the data supports, one of these three failed.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe most damaging misreading is thinking a truncated axis is a styling choice. Starting a bar chart's value axis at 20000 instead of 0 is not zooming in; it visually triples a real gap and misleads anyone who trusts the bars. The zero baseline is mandatory for bars because length is the encoding.\n\nA second misreading is reaching for a pie chart to compare categories. A pie asks the eye to judge angles, which it does poorly, so a four-region revenue comparison that is obvious in a bar chart becomes a guessing game in a pie. Reserve pies for a small number of parts of a single whole, and prefer bars whenever the goal is comparison.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nApply the rules to a different dataset: monthly active users for an app across January through June, rising from 1,200 to 4,100. First state the question: \"How did active users change over the first half of the year?\" That is change over time, so the right chart is a line, not bars; the connected slope shows the growth trend at a glance.\n\nBuild the line from the month and user columns, then check the axis. Because this is a line chart of a trend rather than a magnitude comparison, the zero-baseline rule is softer, but you should still avoid an axis range so tight that normal month-to-month wobble looks like a crisis. Label the vertical axis \"Monthly active users\" so the units are unmistakable.\n\nFinish with a claim-first title: \"Monthly Active Users More Than Tripled, January to June.\" Now the title states the finding, the line is the evidence, and a reader can trace the headline back to the six plotted points.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nA strong reader audits a chart they did not make by asking what trick, if any, is being played. Look first at the axis, then at the chart type, then at the title:\n\n1. Where does the value axis start? If it is above zero on a bar chart, the differences are exaggerated.\n2. Does the chart type fit the question, or is a comparison hidden inside a pie?\n3. Does the title assert a finding the chart actually supports, or does it claim more than the bars show?\n\nWhen you can spot a misleading axis or a mismatched chart type in someone else's figure, you will stop building those mistakes into your own.\n\n<inline-check question=\"A colleague\u2019s bar chart shows West towering over North, but you notice the value axis starts at 20000 instead of 0. What is the real relationship?\" explanation=\"West is 48000 and North is 22000, so West is roughly twice North. A truncated axis starting at 20000 exaggerates the gap, making North nearly vanish. The fix is a zero baseline, because bar length is the visual encoding.\" options='[{\"text\":\"West is about five times North, as the bars show\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"West is about twice North; the axis is exaggerating the gap\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"The chart is fine; truncating the axis is just zooming in\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat100_supplement_charts_that_answer_questions\", \"cat100_supplement_data_storytelling_with_excel\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nA strong chart artifact carries its evidence on its face: a claim-first title, labeled axes with units, a zero baseline where bars demand it, and a source note saying where the numbers came from. The trace should name the question the chart answers and one honest-design choice you made. A model line reads: \"The question was which region led Q1, so I used a bar chart with the axis at 0; the title states 'West Led, More Than Double North,' which a reader can verify against the four bars.\"\n\nThe weak version is a chart with the default title and a hidden truncated axis. The strong version is one where a skeptical reader can confirm the headline claim by reading the bars themselves, with no need to reopen the data.\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nThe same question-first discipline transfers to a relationship chart, where the right form is a scatter rather than a bar. Suppose you want to know whether hours studied relate to exam scores across a class. The question is \"do two numeric variables move together?\", so neither bars nor a line fits; a scatter plot, with hours on the horizontal axis and scores on the vertical, lets the cloud of points reveal whether the relationship is positive, flat, or noisy. Recognizing that this question demands a scatter, and resisting the urge to force it into the familiar bar chart, shows you are matching chart to question rather than defaulting to one shape.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the Excel pivot-table finding with unit labels, source note, and claim-first chart title packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat100-supplement/03-03-charts-that-answer-questions-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"Excel pivot-table finding with unit labels, source note, and claim-first chart title\" course=\"CAT 100\" section=\"03.03\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **NACE career readiness**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the Excel pivot-table finding with unit labels, source note, and claim-first chart title.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the Excel pivot-table finding with unit labels, source note, and claim-first chart title for CAT 100 03.03. A peer comment points to an interface move issue connected to ISTE digital citizenship. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What would make this section more useful while you revise?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"It asks you to name a decision, point to evidence, and choose support that helps the revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"2\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_charts_that_answer_questions\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, check that the Excel pivot-table finding with unit labels, source note, and claim-first chart title records audience, evidence source, AI/software support, accepted revision, rejected suggestion, and confidence change.\n\n## References\n\n- NACE career readiness. https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined\n- ISTE digital citizenship. https://iste.org/standards/students\n- data-ink and visual evidence. https://www.edwardtufte.com/tufte/books_vdqi\n- WCAG accessibility. https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/\n", "simulation": null, "illustration": null, "practice": {"problems": [{"id": "cat100_supplement_3_3_charts_that_answer_questions_artifact_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat100_supplement_charts_that_answer_questions", "concept_ids": ["cat100_supplement_charts_that_answer_questions", "cat100_supplement_data_storytelling_with_excel"], "stem": "You want to show how monthly sales changed across Jan, Feb, Mar, and Apr. Which chart type best answers a 'how did it change over time' question?", "options": ["A pie chart with one slice per month.", "A line chart with months on the horizontal axis and sales on the vertical axis.", "A doughnut chart of the four months.", "A 3-D exploded pie chart for visual interest."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "A line chart maps a value against an ordered axis like time, so the rise and fall across months is easy to read. Pie charts show parts of a whole at one moment, not change over time.", "misconception_id": "cat100_supplement_3_3_charts_that_answer_questions_pie_for_trend", "difficulty": "easy"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_3_3_charts_that_answer_questions_annotation_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat100_supplement_charts_that_answer_questions", "concept_ids": ["cat100_supplement_charts_that_answer_questions"], "stem": "A column chart of donations runs from 2018 to 2024. The vertical axis starts at 90 instead of 0, so a rise from 95 to 100 looks like the bars doubled. What is the honest fix?", "options": ["Keep the axis at 90 because it fills the chart nicely.", "Start the value axis at 0 so the bar heights are proportional to the actual numbers.", "Add more gridlines so the change looks smaller.", "Switch to a darker color so the bars stand out."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "A truncated axis exaggerates small differences. For bar and column charts, starting the value axis at 0 keeps the heights proportional to the data so the reader is not misled.", "misconception_id": "cat100_supplement_3_3_charts_that_answer_questions_truncated_axis", "difficulty": "hard"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_3_3_charts_that_answer_questions_ai_boundary", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat100_supplement_charts_that_answer_questions", "concept_ids": ["cat100_supplement_charts_that_answer_questions", "ai_supported_learning"], "stem": "Your chart is titled 'Sales Chart'. The point you actually found is that the West region outsold every other region. Which title best helps the reader?", "options": ["'Sales Chart' because it names what the chart is.", "'West Region Led Q1 Sales by a Wide Margin' because it states the finding the reader should take away.", "'Figure 3' because it numbers the figure.", "'Data Visualization' because it describes the genre."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "A claim-first title tells the reader the finding, not just the topic. The chart then becomes the evidence for that one stated point.", "misconception_id": "cat100_supplement_3_3_charts_that_answer_questions_topic_not_claim_title", "difficulty": "medium"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_3_3_charts_that_answer_questions_calibration", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat100_supplement_charts_that_answer_questions", "concept_ids": ["cat100_supplement_charts_that_answer_questions", "evidence_based_revision"], "stem": "You need to compare total revenue across five separate stores for one quarter. Which chart answers that comparison most clearly?", "options": ["A line chart connecting the five stores.", "A bar (or column) chart with one bar per store so heights compare directly.", "A pie chart that hides the exact totals.", "A scatter plot of store number against revenue."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "A bar chart compares discrete categories like separate stores by bar height. A line chart implies a connected sequence, which the five unrelated stores do not form.", "misconception_id": "cat100_supplement_3_3_charts_that_answer_questions_line_for_categories", "difficulty": "medium"}]}, "misconceptions": [{"id": "cat100_supplement_3_3_charts_that_answer_questions_pie_for_trend", "pattern": "pie_chart_for_change_over_time", "description": "Using a pie or doughnut chart to show change over time when the question is about a trend across an ordered axis", "trigger": "learner picks a pie chart to display values across months or years", "feedback": "Use a line chart for change over time. Reserve pie charts for parts of a single whole at one moment.", "rail_action": "represent"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_3_3_charts_that_answer_questions_truncated_axis", "pattern": "truncated_value_axis", "description": "Starting a bar or column value axis above 0 so small differences look much larger than they are", "trigger": "learner leaves the value axis truncated on a bar or column chart", "feedback": "Start the value axis of a bar or column chart at 0 so bar heights stay proportional to the actual numbers.", "rail_action": "explain"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_3_3_charts_that_answer_questions_topic_not_claim